{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787125880,"nonce":"Aty/pht7w6lPNdhgJslxfr3XyHg=","message":"✅ **DONE — `db38cef` + migration `00066`.** Closing on the owner's ruling: `frozen` is removed, not wired.\n\nEvery clause of the ruling's replacement Done-when:\n\n- **`frozen` leaves the `channel_threads.state` vocabulary.** Migration `00066` narrows `threads_state_ck` to `('opening','open','dead')` and rewrites `00011:197`'s column comment — the sentence that promised a state nothing could enter, which is what this ticket was filed about.\n- **`Freeze`, `freezeThreadSQL` and the `ThreadStore.Freeze` port method go.** All three deleted. The port keeps a `⛔` note where the method was, in the same shape as the `ClaimStormNotice` deletion note a few lines below it, so the next reader learns why the seam got cheaper rather than wondering what used to be there.\n- **The two tests that were its only callers go.** The fake in `digest_tick_test.go:317` is deleted outright. The other is `clock_test.go`'s `TestThreadWritesSurviveAWorkerBehindTheOneThatOpenedIt`, and the **Freeze step** is what went, not the test: that test is about a worker whose clock lags the pod that opened the thread, and it happened to use `Freeze` as its last write. Deleting the whole test would have dropped clock-skew coverage that has nothing to do with freezing. Its final assertion now reads `ThreadOpen`.\n- **A new migration corrects `00011:197`'s comment.** `00066`, and the new comment says explicitly that there is no `frozen` and why, so the correction does not itself need a reader to go and find this ticket.\n\n⚠️ **Three things beyond what the ticket named, and none was optional.**\n\n1. **A second copy of the vocabulary.** `internal/platform/jobs/ordering/ordering.go:20-21` declares `StateFrozen`, and its own doc comment says the type \"mirrors `channel_threads.state` (threads_state_ck)\". It carried a **live decision arm** at `:186-190` returning `abandon` / `thread_frozen` over an input no row could ever produce. A mirror that declares itself a mirror is part of the vocabulary this ticket says `frozen` leaves, so it went, with its test case.\n2. **The metric label was documented as emittable.** `docs/runbooks/oto_thread_order_decisions_total.md` listed `abandon` / `thread_frozen` as a verdict on `oto_thread_order_decisions_total{action,reason}`. An operator reading that runbook would have waited for a label that can never appear. Removed, along with the SPEC §G.7 decision-table row and the SPEC code snippet that tested `th.State == \"frozen\"`.\n3. **ADR 0023 is annotated, not rewritten.** Its Decision paragraph specifies the gate's evaluation order as `dead → frozen → …`. The decision it records — terminal states first — still stands and is still why `dead` is tested before every waiting condition; only one of its two terminal states is gone. It gets an inline `⛔ SUPERSEDED IN HALF` callout, which is this repo's demonstrated convention (ADR 0020 carries four of them), and the argument above it is left exactly as written because it is the record of what was decided in August 2026.\n\n⚠️ **The one risk, stated rather than hidden.** Narrowing a CHECK can fail: a row already holding `state = 'frozen'` makes `00066` a 23514. No row can hold it through oto — the only writer was `freezeThreadSQL` and only tests ever ran it — so such a failure would mean the value arrived from outside the application, which is worth stopping for. The migration deliberately carries **no** data-fixing `UPDATE`: repairing a row it cannot explain would hide the one fact worth learning.\n\n**The ticket's own open question is answered.** It asked, as \"the first thing to establish\", whether a reply to a closed generation's thread is actually reachable today. It is — and it always was, because nothing ever froze anything. That is the finding, not a gap: the property \"a closed generation stops accepting replies\" was never held by the column added to hold it. Removing the column does not change behaviour; it stops the schema claiming a behaviour oto does not have.\n\n**Gate:** all eleven `ci` recipes individually. 9/11 green; the two reds are exactly the two pre-existing baselines (`lint-vocabulary` at 60, unchanged, and `TestNoStateHueInChrome` from `59c7def`) and nothing else — one `--- FAIL` line in the whole suite. `test/integration` passed, so `TestEveryMigrationDownTo00028IsReversible` exercised `00066` in **both** directions against a real database, including that the Down re-admits `frozen` so the release it rolls back to can still write it.","files":null}]}